  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{(x-1)({x}^{3}+1)}{{x}^{2}-1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)({x}^{3}+1)}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}-x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}-x+1)}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/c76cce145059193c5c5348f2de347ac470f35e8b5392e196ac8b9766382bf2b8.png)",
